Emergency contraception or oral contraceptives the next morning is a way to prevent pregnancy if a woman forgets to take birth control pills and the partner does not use a condom or a torn condom or is forced to have sex.
How does this medicine work?
It is usually taken 72 hours after sex.
After taking this medicine, women can usually start menstruating in the next 2 or 3 weeks.
It acts to inhibit the secretion of sperm, prevent the two sexes from meeting each other and alter the lining of the uterus to prevent fertilization.
What are the types of emergency contraceptive pills?
These drugs are approved by the US Food and Drug Administration.
How effective are emergency contraception?
Emergency contraception does not guarantee 100% effectiveness.
Sometimes women can use it late after unprotected sex.
Experts advise women to return to the antenatal clinic in the third week to make sure she is not pregnant.
Who can take this medicine?
Not all women can take emergency contraception.
Giving this medicine to a woman needs to focus on the details of her past and present health.
The timing of unprotected sex also needs to be recorded.
It can not be used in women with hemorrhoids, atherosclerosis, severe liver disease, unexplained vaginal bleeding, suspected cancer and pregnancy.
In rare cases, women taking the drug may be at risk for hemorrhage, stroke, and heart attack, especially women over the age of 35 who smoke regularly.
What are the possible side effects of this medicine?
Common side effects can include headaches, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tightness of the breasts, and irregular bleeding.
Some studies have also noted that women who become pregnant unknowingly and take emergency contraception can have more problems with their unborn baby.
The baby is prone to developmental problems with reproductive organs, chest or arm pain, shortness of breath, leg swelling, severe headaches, conjunctivitis, jaundice, yellow eyes, abdominal pain and some mental illness.
Do emergency contraception require the help of a doctor?
