Penis cancer symptoms are easily confused with STIs
The risk of penile cancer has increased by about 20 percent in the last 30 years, according to a new study.
As for the symptoms of penile cancer, it can confuse men with the symptoms of STIs.
On the other hand, penile cancer can occur anywhere on the penis, but most often on the skin of the penis and the head of the penis.
The cause of penile cancer, while the exact cause of penile cancer is not clear, but experts have identified three major factors that can put men at risk for this deadly disease.
These include poor hygiene or protection against infections, circumcision, and smoking.
More than 100 HPV types are commonly observed, with only two groups being linked to penile cancer.
The same two groups of viruses can also cause other cancers, such as cervical cancer, cervical cancer and anal cancer.
Men who smoke are more likely to develop penile cancer.
Men who have not had their genitals amputated or the tip of their penis amputated are also at higher risk for developing penile cancer.
The reason why men who do not cut the penis or cut the tip of the penis can be very risky because they find it difficult to pull the skin of the penis to clean the head of their genitals.
It is this lack of hygiene that promotes the transmission of viruses, making men more vulnerable to penile cancer.
The other two factors that cause a person to get penile cancer is that the man develops a serious skin condition that can lead to severe skin rashes and weakened immune system through the use of certain medications.
How to protect yourself from penile cancer To protect yourself from penile cancer, men should use a condom during sex to avoid getting HPV.
Circumcision can also reduce the risk of this disease.
If not, men should get in the habit of pulling the foreskin to the base of the penis to properly cleanse their genitals.
If any of the symptoms are noticeable, men should consult a doctor immediately, as treatment for the early stages may have a high recovery rate.
