Breast milk contains different substances depending on the sex of the baby
A study by biologists from Harvard Kathy Hindey University found that mothers' milk production to feed their sons and daughters was different.
According to the above study, which focused on humans using monkeys and other mammals, mothers produced milk for their sons and daughters in different quantities and quantities. .
Research figures show that mothers' sons produce more milk, or more protein, than daughters.
This evidence has been evident since the baby was in the womb.
Biologists from Harvard University studied 1.49 million cows for 305 days.
They found that an average cow could produce 445 kilograms of milk while producing a calf, which is higher than the milk produced when a cow gives birth to a calf.
The results of this research could be key in the production of various types of milk powder to meet the needs in accordance with the nutritional factors and sex of the baby.
