Common Digestive Disorders:
Gas, bloating and irregularities:
Gas formation is a simple part of digestion, except in excess, which usually indicates the absorption of fiber and its proper function in digestion.
Some foods that contain carbohydrates and soy products are not broken down during digestion, so the bacteria that live in your gut are responsible for producing gas to complete the digestive process.
On average, people send out gas about 14 times a day, releasing about half a liter of gas during the digestive process.
Some people describe the feeling of bloating after eating, it is a feeling of bloating that occurs as a little faster, mostly in the upper abdomen.
Often it is the result of the air remaining in the digestive tract being surprisingly large.
It is usually a matter of swallowing a lot of air when you eat, which often happens if you eat too fast or talk too much while chewing.
Sometimes carbonated beverages can make you feel bloated from the moment you swallow a lot of air with your drink.
Some people experience bloating when they eat a lot of fatty foods, slowing down the digestion of the stomach, which makes you feel uncomfortable.
Irregularities are a common complaint of digestion and are also misleading.
Some people think that if they do not go to the bathroom every day, they will have problems.
But if it goes smoothly, whether three times a day or three times a week, you probably have nothing to worry about.
My 7 Ways to Keep Your Digestion Healthy and Eat Enough Fiber:
Fiber is part of the structure of plants, so it is found in rich foods such as fruits, beans and whole grains.
Adolescents should eat about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average intake of teens in the United States is only about a third of that amount.
Our busy lifestyle leads to problems When we eat on the road, we are less likely to find fruits, vegetables and whole grains that are rich in fiber.
Look for vegetables and fruits to go with every meal and snack, put beans in soups and salads, and choose whole grains with a selection of white nuts, breads, cereals, rice and pasta.
Get some good bacteria:
Your digestive system is home to thousands of beneficial bacteria that help break down food that is normally resistant to digestion, allowing you to absorb nutrients from your diet.
Bacteria in your digestive system also help maintain the growth of other bacteria, potentially damaging them, thus making your digestion healthier.
While the use of bacteria in the diet has not been disputed, the probiotic bacteria contained in yogurt and processed foods can help improve digestive health.
Meet the need for fluids Fluids help to increase the amount of fiber in the diet and increase the volume of the body through the weak digestive tract, which makes the body travel smoothly.
Fruits and vegetables that are rich in water can meet the need for fluids, but still need to eat fluids daily.
Exercise regularly Exercise is not only good for building muscle, as you can see, it is also good for the soft muscles of your digestive tract.
Exercise helps muscles to contract, which is why muscles can move smoothly.
Exercise can also reduce stress, which is good for people with a dysfunctional digestive system to regenerate once they have relieved stress.
Take your time .... Change your diet often When people are bothered by gas, they suggest that the best way is to cut down on fatty foods such as beans or protein from the diet.
But trying to eat small amounts for three to four days to allow time for your digestive system to adjust is better than stopping eating all of these healthy foods.
Similarly, if you try to add fiber to your diet, follow the instructions, eating 25 to 30 grams in sequence.
Take your time .... Eat and work out When you eat too fast, not only does it impair your appetite for food, but you seem to inhale the air that leads to gas and bloating.
And when you eat too fast, you will be able to eat more than your brain has determined that your stomach needs 20 minutes to fill up, leading to poor digestion.
Finally, when nature calls for sure, you must listen.
