People with glaucoma should sleep on one side of the eye.
NEW YORK (Reuters Health) - People with glaucoma should not lie on their side of the eye, according to a new study by South Korean researchers.
According to Dr. Jeffrey Scalz, lying on your back with the above conditions creates high blood pressure, which can lead to glaucoma, which can make the problem worse.
According to the World Health Organization, glaucoma, or glaucoma, can lead to paralysis after cataracts.
More than 66 percent of patients with moderate glaucoma reported having a habit of lying with their head tilted in the direction of the affected eye.
More than 71 percent of patients with severe glaucoma also have the same reclining habit.
The study recommended that patients lie on their side, facing the direction of the good eye or eyes, rather than resting on the sore eye.
Patients can also sleep on their backs, but if they sleep on their backs, it can cause insomnia, which can make glaucoma worse.
