Eating ice can lead to obesity and diabetes
NEW YORK (Reuters Health) - Consumption of frozen drinks, cold drinks and frozen foods may increase the risk of obesity and diabetes, according to a new study.
Cold temperatures make our tongues numb, not easily recognizing the sweet taste.
That is why we are prone to overeating when eating cold foods or iced drinks.
Researchers at the University of Arkansas in the United States conducted experiments by delivering water at different temperatures to hundreds of people.
People who drink cold water with ice feel that the chocolate is not sweet, while those who drink hot water feel that the chocolate tastes sweet.
Researchers believe that cold drinks or ice can make people taste better than food.
They concluded that people who regularly drank ice and cold drinks were more likely to eat sweets, which made them more likely to gain weight and develop diabetes.
