Scientists test candy that does not cause tooth decay
Dentists and parents often forbid their children from eating too much candy for fear of tooth decay.
But this stain will be a thing of the past because scientists have just discovered a type of candy that contains good bacteria and does not harm the teeth.
In fact, candy is not the cause of tooth decay, it is the bacteria that lurk in the human mouth that destroy our teeth.
When you eat candy, those bacteria begin to release acid, which damages the gums and causes tooth decay.
This new bacterium can attach to bacteria in the teeth, making it less likely to react with acid to damage human teeth.
A study of 60 people found that people who ate new candy were less likely to develop tooth decay than before.
