Watching too much TV makes children grow up to be overweight
Adult weight gain may be caused by watching too many hours of television when they were children, a new study suggests.
Getting your child to watch television for hours each day is likely to have a negative effect on adulthood.
One hour of television viewing for a child is equivalent to gaining about half a kilogram of weight when the child is an adult, according to a new study.
The researchers believe that this is due to the fact that while children are watching TV, they are not idle, and they often grab everything around them to eat as a treat.
These are bad habits while children are addicted to television.
Dr. Jennifer Felby, head of the research team at Harvard University School of Public Health in Boston, USA, said:
It is not a change that is good for their health.
In general, while watching TV, children often use their hands to find snacks around them to eat, while eating while watching TV like this is not conducive to health.
On the other hand, long hours of watching TV also indicate a child's inactivity and lack of physical activity.
As a result, such lifestyle habits can influence children to become overweight adults.
According to US and Canadian health experts, children should limit their television viewing to no more than two hours a day.
Breast cancer is a cancer that can affect any woman from puberty onwards.
Many factors contribute to the development of this disease, but the main characteristics of the disease are related to genes that are transmitted from the mother or his lineage.
It is observed that the incidence of breast cancer is high in women who have mothers, aunts or uncles have cancer, especially if they have cancer before the age of 50.
In addition to genetic transmission, we have observed that lifestyle and diet also contribute to the development of breast cancer.
Here are 10 things we can do to reduce our risk of breast cancer.
Avoid excess weight. The occurrence of obesity can increase the risk of breast cancer, especially after menopause.
This is an important time in a woman's life when cancer is most common.
Healthy diet We should eat foods that are rich in fiber and fruits and vegetables.
Drinks should be low in sugar and women should be especially careful with foods that contain fatty acids.
Women should reduce their intake of red meat and increase protein-rich foods such as fish, chicken breast, whole grains and vegetable oils rather than animal fats.
Work and play sports Many studies have shown that regular exercise or sports can reduce the incidence of cancer by 10 to 30 percent in older people.
Exercise includes regular walking or running for at least 30 minutes five days a week.
Reduce alcohol. Regular consumption of alcohol is an contributing factor that increases the risk of breast cancer.
Women should reduce their alcohol consumption or avoid alcohol altogether.
Avoid hormone replacement therapy The hormone replacement therapy during menopause increases the risk of breast cancer.
However, if a woman needs to take hormones to treat postmenopausal problems, she should avoid any hormone supplements that contain progesterone and should limit her use to no longer overdose. 3 years.
Quit smoking Research has shown that long-term smoking is a contributing factor in increasing the risk of breast cancer in some women.
Breastfeeding If possible, women should breastfeed for at least a year, and this can significantly reduce the risk of breast cancer.
Monitor information about the disease Regular participation in the activities of associations or NGOs working on breast cancer research can enable women to stay informed about the results of new research and to take advantage of that information. Take care of your own health as well.
