Condoms can help keep good bacteria, protect the vagina
Condoms: In addition to the benefits of preventing infertility and sexually transmitted infections, it can also help prevent bacteria in the vagina from working properly.
This is according to a new study by a Chinese research team.
The new study found that women who used condoms were more likely to prevent microbes from growing in the vagina than women who used contraception and other diseases.
By maintaining good bacteria, it can balance the acidity in the vagina and prevent vaginal itching and other ailments.
Researchers studied 164 healthy married women at the Beijing Friendship Hospital in China between the ages of 18 and 45.
Of them, 72 used condoms on a regular basis, 57 used other contraceptives and 35 used extravagant injections and calendar sex.
The researchers noted that women who used condoms had higher levels of good bacteria in the vaginal area and were less likely to develop genital herpes or cuts.
The results of this study show that the use of condoms not only helps women avoid infection and accidental pregnancy, it also maintains acid balance and creates good bacteria to protect the vagina.
