These features are related to skin thickness, skin pigmentation and subcutaneous fat.
Thickness and skin color
Asians usually have thicker skin on their bodies than Europeans and more often have skin problems after an injury or after surgery.
Therefore, all doctors should find out and avoid any unnecessary surgery to avoid the occurrence of this fruit.
On the other hand, our Asian skin is less wrinkled compared to Europeans, especially the darkening of the skin is mild compared to the skin on Europeans exposed to the same sunlight.
Asians therefore have a lower risk of developing skin cancer due to sun exposure than Europeans.
Subcutaneous fat
In Asians, the accumulation of fat cells under the skin, especially around the jaw, near the nose, or around the lips, and under the chin, was lower than in Europeans of a similar age.
Therefore, the reduction of subcutaneous fat is minimal in Asians.
Skeleton and subcutaneous muscle layer
The bone structure on the face of Asians is completely different from that of Europeans.
Many Asian women have high cheekbones and low chins, and our jaw bones are much larger, which we call tiger jaws, making our faces square.
Asians' subcutaneous muscles are usually thicker than those of European women, leading to subcutaneous muscle spasms or sagging muscles that are more noticeable.
