Discover Acne Treatment Mistakes
Acne is a skin disease that affects the sebaceous glands of our body, especially on the face.
The most common causes of sebaceous glands, which are common to everyone, are the effects of hormonal changes during puberty and diet.
Clogged pores and infection can cause acne to turn into pus, which can lead to many side effects, such as scarring, scarring, and darkening of the skin after healing.
Although acne is mild in the early stages, it needs to be treated properly by a specialist.
A study in Thailand found that only 15 percent of acne sufferers were treated properly, with the rest being treated with medication.
Due to the wrong treatment, this can lead to side effects that are sometimes almost impossible to change.
Here are some common mistakes related to acne treatment without consulting a specialist.
Patients often blame treatment that does not see immediate results, as he does not understand that the disease requires 2 to 6 weeks.
In the early stages of treatment, the drug can cause redness, but it is not an infection but an inflammation.
Most patients stop self-medication and seek a new one.
Remember that each medicine has different features, and in some cases, a combination of two or more drugs can cause severe skin damage.
The use of alkaline substances, alcohol or sunscreen can cause the pores to become clogged, causing the treatment to fail.
This can slow down the healing process.
Acne can sometimes cause serious skin problems and other infections, such as hepatitis or AIDS.
Waiting too long or trying out medications before seeing a specialist can make acne more difficult to treat or leave side effects.
Stopping treatment immediately just to see relief may make the acne recur and make it harder to treat later.
